[Music]  nature is here at cop26 our reporters  have been closely following all of the  tense climate negotiations  on the final day we sat them down to  discuss what they had found out and what  we should be looking forward to when the  final text comes in hello we're nature  reporters we are here at cop 26 in  glasgow it's the final day in my opinion  i feel like the atmosphere is really  electric today i've never seen anything  like it  was my first cop and what struck me most  is the very enormity of the event it's  been really overwhelming  it's partly perhaps because we've been  doing our jobs for the last 18 months  our normal reporting jobs from small  bedrooms little offices front rooms and  all of a sudden from doing that to being  thrust in this sort of ginormous  international  convention center has been quite  disorienting i would absolutely agree  it's huge it's overwhelming but you  don't really see any of that from the  outside there are so many things that  you experience when you're inside this  village that aren't really worthy of  inclusion in a news story but actually  all feeds into  that feeling of either  optimism or pessimism that you might  come away with and at the end of the day  just to interact with people who are  really trying to grapple with these big  problems um in all sorts of ways from  whether technology based or from the  perspective of social sciences or from  the perspective of science-based policy  uh it does help uh you know keep me in a  positive frame of mind  but i understand queer and you've been  talking to scientists who are here and  observing from the outside  what are they thinking about how this  meeting is going on  i mean the cop is by definition a  political meeting  and there's such a it is not a science  meeting  but still the issue of climate and  global change is now  an issue  that encompasses all science no  discipline of science can afford anymore  to ignore  the climate change challenges  that uh the world is is facing and the  scientists that you're talking to about  this meeting what would you say is their  feeling so far is it optimistic hopeful  skeptical  that depends of course on whom  you ask i have not experienced a lot of  you know deep-filled pessimism here  of course scientists  are well aware of the challenges ahead  but i think being scientists  they  already and and happy uh to facing these  challenges now essen i know you've been  looking into the climate justice side of  the equation i spoke to uh five maybe  six of the  researchers who were very involved in  the early 90s  in the rio earth summit and  they're a bit depressed  they feel that  the process  that they were all part of  set and train  um  many things  um which  have either been forgotten or they've  been deliberately sort of obfuscated and  avoided  one of those things is the question of  historical emissions  which we don't hear about and we haven't  really heard about so much in the cop  which is there was a study that we  reported on from the center for science  and environment in new delhi where the  researchers  added up all the emissions from roughly  the industrial revolution to now and of  course even  by the standards of  future decarbonization there's still a  whacking great amount that's occupied by  just seven or eight countries including  china but mostly what they call the  historic limiters  the argument they're making is that  there needs to be much sharper and  quicker decarbonization from the richer  countries in the next eight or nine  years otherwise the global carbon budget  just won't  accommodate  their industrial needs  and of course all the debate now here at  the cop is everyone has to decarbonize  and so they feel these architects feel  that  what they called common but  differentiated responsibilities everyone  has a responsibility but they're  different  that language  has gone historical emissions has gone  and that sort of concerns them  and what about the funding that we had  perhaps expected or planned to see flow  from the richer nations to the  developing world it's very likely that  there's going to be movement on funding  and there is a a pledge of 100 billion  the pinch point is that it's mostly  loans  which is not  what was envisioned back in the early  days of these processes was meant to be  that the phrasing was new and additional  not money that makes  poor countries even more poorer because  they get into debt so that's a worry  and there is talk also here of what they  call loss and damage it's really  a liability and compensation in the  event that a poor country you know has a  really hard time because of emissions  that it didn't do  but they don't use the phrasing of  liability and compensation because that  could open themselves up to legal claims  so they call it loss and damage  and there is language around loss and  damage in the text  i think the language of the agreement is  something that i've learned a lot about  which is that although these documents  are short they  lack  detail  each word each phrase is fought over and  is really important and i think  for from the outsider perspective  uh it might seem you know it might seem  a lot of discussion over what is really  a handful of sentences at the end of the  day but each one of those phrases  whether it be loss and damage uh or  something else can then be used uh  that's basically thin end of the wedge  that all the policy makers and decision  makers then use to push these decisions  forward so what key phrases do you think  we should be looking for either in their  presence or their absence  in the discussion i know one i was  looking for  was in uh decarbonizing transport uh  there was a lot of  discussion over whether active travel  which basically means cycling and  walking and not just electric cars would  make it into the discussion and did make  it as a little note at the end so what  can we tell our readers to look at oh um  i would say  two maybe three so look out for coal so  at the moment uh there is text  that  is saying something to the effect of  coal being phased out  um there'll be all kinds of caveats to  that then there's the uh the double f  word  fossil fuels this is in the context of  subsidies in the sense that there may be  a call for subsidies public subsidies  fossil fuels to be phased out now some  countries particularly the oil producing  states a country like saudi arabia or  iran for example parts of the u.s  russia it's the public sector that  essentially is the owner of the fossil  fuel industry so you could argue that by  definition that is entirely a subsidy  so if you keep the word subsidy in there  then their economies like crash  overnight all that so that's what their  worry is  of course everybody will look for  specific commitments financial  commitments and commitments as to  the speed of decarbonization  but as long as  governments are unable to tell us how  they will actually achieve their  ambitions  scientists and the general public will  tend to think that anything what  governments may say is is but lip  service so a term that i've learned  during this cough is this term non-state  factors so we've been talking about uh  funding for coal for fossil fuels uh  subsidies uh this is all really within  the public sphere but there's a renewed  focus on the decarbonization that has to  happen in private industry and  corporation now i know the u.n has  announced a couple expert panels where  researchers are going to be providing  oversight can you tell us a bit more  about that so yeah so this is in the  context of an announcement by business  businesses are  saying or claiming uh that potentially  many many tens of trillions of dollars  will move from  their existing investments into what  they are calling netzero investments  so what the un has gone and done uh they  have they announced more or less the  same time that they would set up um some  scientists who would start to assess and  evaluate what these commitments really  mean in practice  it's very early days they haven't even  announced the names of the scientists  we're trying to find that out that  information now but that's where we are  but outside of these halls where we're  sitting there's a lot of  um  suggestions such as green washing my  feeling is that most scientists would  be rather cautious about  you know the benefit of  setting up just another  expert panel of course it is important  to have to get people to talk with each  other and have scientists among  the people  tasked with making decisions  but don't trust on panels so  the big question  is cop 26  going to move the needle  on climate change  and if so to the extent that it's  necessary  assad  um  yeah it will move the needle um question  is how far and how fast  um so there's all to play for in the  next 24 hours next 24 to 48 hours but  then there's also a long game after i  mean in a way the real work happens  beyond uh beyond this uh this meeting  and what do you think we're in are we  gonna be walking out of the door  celebrating tomorrow morning  um yes and no um  this  cop was not a game changer nobody  expected this i guess the challenge  ahead now from a from a scientific  perspective  is to  bring together the various sciences  and to make them  work  and act upon the problem in a meaningful  way um and how  this might happen when so many  disciplines are involved is an open  question but there's growing interest  among scientists and there's enthusiasm  among scientists to tackle the problem  so um from  a scientific  point of view i think we can leave this  meeting with some uh optimistic feelings  i mean i will chime in that i have been  trying to  see what's going on outside the  conference center as well as inside and  for me  we absolutely cannot ignore  all of the people who are not maybe  coming through the security gates but  are very very passionately engaged with  what's happening here and  i think that the impact of these  activists  really can't be overstated in terms of  how  uh how the decision makers are  responding there's a feeling that the  decisions that they make will directly  impact their own careers and that's i  think what is very motivating for people  so  for me the  whatever happens inside the walls  there's a fundamental change going on  outside the walls as well that hopefully  will contribute to  positive movement going forward

